    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# SYSTEM ROLE & TASK OVERVIEW\nAct as a Principal Full-Stack AI Engineer and Software Architect. I want to build a web application called \"AI Consensus Hub\" (or \"DebateAI\"). \n\n### PROJECT CORE CONCEPT\nAn interactive web application where multiple AI models (emulating ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Groq/Llama, and a web-searching Perplexity-style agent) receive a user\'s single prompt, debate each other in structured rounds, cross-critique their arguments, and dynamically synthesize the absolute best, highly verified consensus answer.\n\n### CRITICAL CONSTRAINT: $0 BUDGET\nThe entire tech stack, model API usage, database, and hosting MUST be 100% FREE forever (no paid tiers, no credit cards required, using free developer tiers only).\n\n---\n\n## 1. TECHNICAL STACK ARCHITECTURE\n\nProvide full design choices adhering strictly to free tools:\n1. **Frontend**: Next.js 14+ (App Router) with Tailwind CSS, TypeScript, and Lucide Icons for clean UI components.\n2. **Backend**: Next.js Server Actions or FastAPI (Python) for asynchronous pipeline handling.\n3. **Model Integration / Gateways**:\n   - Google Gemini API via Google AI Studio Free Tier (Gemini 2.5 Flash / Pro)\n   - Groq API (High-speed Llama 3.3 70B / Gemma models)\n   - OpenRouter API (Free models like `:free` endpoints)\n   - Search Agent (DuckDuckGo Search SDK or Tavily Free API + Free LLM to emulate Perplexity)\n4. **Database & Caching (Optional/Free)**: Supabase (PostgreSQL free tier) to cache previous debate results and prevent burning rate limits on duplicate queries.\n5. **Deployment**: Vercel Free Hobby Tier.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DEBATE WORKFLOW ENGINE DESIGN\n\nExplain and code the logic for a 3-Step Orchestration Pipeline:\n\n### Step 1: Parallel Generation (Draft Phase)\n- Accept user prompt.\n- Send the prompt simultaneously (`Promise.all` or `asyncio.gather`) to 3 distinct LLM endpoints (e.g., Gemini, Groq/Llama-3.3, and Search-Grounded Agent).\n- Restrict max output tokens to ~250 words per agent to save API limits.\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Critique & Convergence Phase\n- Feed all Step 1 responses into each model with a system prompt:\n  \"Review these responses from other models: [Response A], [Response B]. Point out logical flaws, missing edge cases, or hallucinations, and provide your updated, refined position.\"\n- Run models in parallel to produce updated drafts.\n\n### Step 3: Synthesis & Verdict (Judge Phase)\n- Feed the initial prompt, all original drafts, and all cross-critiques into the strongest model (e.g., Gemini 2.5 Pro or Llama-3.3-70B on Groq).\n- Output:\n  1. Final Consensus Answer (The ultimate answer).\n  2. Key Agreed Points.\n  3. Divergent/Disagreed Points.\n  4. Confidence Score (1\342\200\223100%).\n\n---\n\n## 3. UI/UX SPECIFICATIONS\n\nDesign a sleek, modern UI layout (similar to Perplexity/Claude/ChatGPT) containing:\n1. **Input Section**: Clean prompt bar with toggle options (e.g., Select Debate Intensity: Fast 1-Round vs. Deep 2-Round Debate).\n2. **Real-time Progress Tracker**: Visual stepper showing:\n   - `[Phase 1: Querying Models...]`\n   - `[Phase 2: Agents Debating & Critiquing...]`\n   - `[Phase 3: Synthesizing Final Consensus...]`\n3. **Debate Arena View**: Tabbed or side-by-side card UI displaying each model\'s initial stance, their critique of others, and their revised stance.\n4. **Final Consensus Card**: A highlighted, primary card displaying the synthesized master answer with collapsible sources/arguments.\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHAT YOU NEED TO PROVIDE NOW\n\nPlease act as the developer and generate:\n1. **System Architecture Blueprint**: Clean ASCII flow chart or markdown breakdown of the data pipeline.\n2. **Environment Variables Template (`.env.local`)**: Listing all required free keys (Google AI Studio, Groq, OpenRouter, etc.).\n3. **Core Backend Orchestrator Code**: A production-ready TypeScript/Next.js API route or Python module implementing the async multi-agent debate loop with error handling (fallback if one API hits a rate limit).\n4. **Frontend UI Component**: React / Next.js Tailwind code for displaying the live multi-agent debate and consensus cards cleanly.\n5. **Rate-Limit & Optimization Strategies**: Step-by-step guidance on how to avoid hitting free API tier limits (requests per minute/day).\n\nLet\'s begin step by step. Start with the System Architecture Blueprint and the Core Pipeline Code!"
    items {
      text: "# SYSTEM ROLE & TASK OVERVIEW\nAct as a Principal Full-Stack AI Engineer and Software Architect. I want to build a web application called \"AI Consensus Hub\" (or \"DebateAI\"). \n\n### PROJECT CORE CONCEPT\nAn interactive web application where multiple AI models (emulating ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Groq/Llama, and a web-searching Perplexity-style agent) receive a user\'s single prompt, debate each other in structured rounds, cross-critique their arguments, and dynamically synthesize the absolute best, highly verified consensus answer.\n\n### CRITICAL CONSTRAINT: $0 BUDGET\nThe entire tech stack, model API usage, database, and hosting MUST be 100% FREE forever (no paid tiers, no credit cards required, using free developer tiers only).\n\n---\n\n## 1. TECHNICAL STACK ARCHITECTURE\n\nProvide full design choices adhering strictly to free tools:\n1. **Frontend**: Next.js 14+ (App Router) with Tailwind CSS, TypeScript, and Lucide Icons for clean UI components.\n2. **Backend**: Next.js Server Actions or FastAPI (Python) for asynchronous pipeline handling.\n3. **Model Integration / Gateways**:\n   - Google Gemini API via Google AI Studio Free Tier (Gemini 2.5 Flash / Pro)\n   - Groq API (High-speed Llama 3.3 70B / Gemma models)\n   - OpenRouter API (Free models like `:free` endpoints)\n   - Search Agent (DuckDuckGo Search SDK or Tavily Free API + Free LLM to emulate Perplexity)\n4. **Database & Caching (Optional/Free)**: Supabase (PostgreSQL free tier) to cache previous debate results and prevent burning rate limits on duplicate queries.\n5. **Deployment**: Vercel Free Hobby Tier.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DEBATE WORKFLOW ENGINE DESIGN\n\nExplain and code the logic for a 3-Step Orchestration Pipeline:\n\n### Step 1: Parallel Generation (Draft Phase)\n- Accept user prompt.\n- Send the prompt simultaneously (`Promise.all` or `asyncio.gather`) to 3 distinct LLM endpoints (e.g., Gemini, Groq/Llama-3.3, and Search-Grounded Agent).\n- Restrict max output tokens to ~250 words per agent to save API limits.\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Critique & Convergence Phase\n- Feed all Step 1 responses into each model with a system prompt:\n  \"Review these responses from other models: [Response A], [Response B]. Point out logical flaws, missing edge cases, or hallucinations, and provide your updated, refined position.\"\n- Run models in parallel to produce updated drafts.\n\n### Step 3: Synthesis & Verdict (Judge Phase)\n- Feed the initial prompt, all original drafts, and all cross-critiques into the strongest model (e.g., Gemini 2.5 Pro or Llama-3.3-70B on Groq).\n- Output:\n  1. Final Consensus Answer (The ultimate answer).\n  2. Key Agreed Points.\n  3. Divergent/Disagreed Points.\n  4. Confidence Score (1\342\200\223100%).\n\n---\n\n## 3. UI/UX SPECIFICATIONS\n\nDesign a sleek, modern UI layout (similar to Perplexity/Claude/ChatGPT) containing:\n1. **Input Section**: Clean prompt bar with toggle options (e.g., Select Debate Intensity: Fast 1-Round vs. Deep 2-Round Debate).\n2. **Real-time Progress Tracker**: Visual stepper showing:\n   - `[Phase 1: Querying Models...]`\n   - `[Phase 2: Agents Debating & Critiquing...]`\n   - `[Phase 3: Synthesizing Final Consensus...]`\n3. **Debate Arena View**: Tabbed or side-by-side card UI displaying each model\'s initial stance, their critique of others, and their revised stance.\n4. **Final Consensus Card**: A highlighted, primary card displaying the synthesized master answer with collapsible sources/arguments.\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHAT YOU NEED TO PROVIDE NOW\n\nPlease act as the developer and generate:\n1. **System Architecture Blueprint**: Clean ASCII flow chart or markdown breakdown of the data pipeline.\n2. **Environment Variables Template (`.env.local`)**: Listing all required free keys (Google AI Studio, Groq, OpenRouter, etc.).\n3. **Core Backend Orchestrator Code**: A production-ready TypeScript/Next.js API route or Python module implementing the async multi-agent debate loop with error handling (fallback if one API hits a rate limit).\n4. **Frontend UI Component**: React / Next.js Tailwind code for displaying the live multi-agent debate and consensus cards cleanly.\n5. **Rate-Limit & Optimization Strategies**: Step-by-step guidance on how to avoid hitting free API tier limits (requests per minute/day).\n\nLet\'s begin step by step. Start with the System Architecture Blueprint and the Core Pipeline Code!"
    }
  }
}
